SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National survey shows progress in breastfeeding, adolescent births and violence, but worrying trends in education and child development","publisher":"UNICEF Thailand","publication_date":"2023-07-10","url":"https://www.unicef.org/thailand/press-releases/national-survey-shows-progress-breastfeeding-adolescent-births-and-violence-worrying","claim":"MICS 2022 collected data from 34,000 households across Thailand between June and October 2022 and the findings are intended to help policymakers and government agencies shape policy and monitor the SDGs.","locator":"paras. 67-69 and 107-108","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF is the survey partner and the release describes direct 2022 fieldwork and policy use.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 fieldwork with publication in 2023; strong period fit.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable only in part to the Prayut-led government's policy environment and cooperation with NSO/UNICEF; the survey itself is descriptive and also reflects broader conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The same release also reports serious education, nutrition, and child-marriage setbacks, so the overall picture is mixed.","Survey findings are not by themselves proof of ruler performance."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National survey shows progress in breastfeeding, adolescent births and violence, but worrying trends in education and child development","publisher":"UNICEF Thailand","publication_date":"2023-07-10","url":"https://www.unicef.org/thailand/press-releases/national-survey-shows-progress-breastfeeding-adolescent-births-and-violence-worrying","claim":"MICS 2022 found improved exclusive breastfeeding, lower adolescent birth rates, and less violent discipline at home compared with prior rounds.","locator":"paras. 70-71","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct survey reporting from UNICEF and NSO on 2022 data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 outcome data published shortly after the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Limited attribution to Prayut: this is a national outcome that may reflect preexisting trends, local actors, and household behavior as much as central policy.","contrary_evidence":["The same release notes that child development and education worsened after the pandemic.","Positive changes are partial and do not establish broad institutional success."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National survey shows progress in breastfeeding, adolescent births and violence, but worrying trends in education and child development","publisher":"UNICEF Thailand","publication_date":"2023-07-10","url":"https://www.unicef.org/thailand/press-releases/national-survey-shows-progress-breastfeeding-adolescent-births-and-violence-worrying","claim":"MICS 2022 shows worse early-childhood attendance, lower pre-primary participation, more children out of school, and weaker reading and numeracy skills than in 2019.","locator":"paras. 72-79","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Directly reported survey results from the national statistical system and UNICEF.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 field data with 2023 release, matching the target year closely.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the Prayut-era policy environment only indirectly; it is a system outcome with limited direct ruler attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The release also notes some improvements in breastfeeding and adolescent births.","Pandemic disruption is an important confounder."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"National survey shows progress in breastfeeding, adolescent births and violence, but worrying trends in education and child development","publisher":"UNICEF Thailand","publication_date":"2023-07-10","url":"https://www.unicef.org/thailand/press-releases/national-survey-shows-progress-breastfeeding-adolescent-births-and-violence-worrying","claim":"MICS 2022 reports that 25 percent of children under 18 were not living with their parents and that inequality persists by region, household wealth, maternal education, and ethnicity, with data also covering access to social transfers.","locator":"paras. 83-84","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct survey evidence on distribution and child living arrangements.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 household survey data.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is partial and indirect; these are structural social outcomes and household conditions not fully controlled by the national ruler.","contrary_evidence":["The same survey also shows some gains in breastfeeding and violence reduction.","Household separation often reflects internal migration and family structure, not only state policy."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Five impacts of COVID-19 on children in Thailand","publisher":"UNICEF Thailand","publication_date":"2022-07-22","url":"https://www.unicef.org/thailand/stories/five-impacts-covid-19-children-thailand","claim":"UNICEF reports that around 60 percent of low-income households and households with women and children ran out of food in the first year of the pandemic, and about 400,000 more people fell below the poverty line in 2020.","locator":"paras. 76-88","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF article summarizing Thailand-specific research on child and household welfare during the pandemic.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and describing pandemic effects that still shaped the 2022 welfare environment.","ruler_attribution":"This is partly attributable to the Prayut-era pandemic response and social protection system, but also to COVID shock and inherited baseline conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The article does not isolate Prayut-specific decisions from pandemic conditions.","It mainly shows harm and need, not complete policy failure or success by itself."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Five impacts of COVID-19 on children in Thailand","publisher":"UNICEF Thailand","publication_date":"2022-07-22","url":"https://www.unicef.org/thailand/stories/five-impacts-covid-19-children-thailand","claim":"School closures affected almost 9 million children, many low-income children lacked computer or electricity access, and only 86 percent of low-income children aged 6-17 were enrolled in school in 2021 because of costs and COVID fears.","locator":"paras. 90-93","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Concrete Thailand-specific welfare and education access data from UNICEF.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during the target year and directly relevant to 2022 social welfare conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution to Prayut is indirect but meaningful through pandemic-era education and welfare response capacity; the article also reflects shock effects beyond the ruler's control.","contrary_evidence":["The article emphasizes crisis damage rather than durable government improvement.","These are pandemic-era outcomes, not proof of long-run ruler competence."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand’s migrant vaccination programme ensures no-one is left behind","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-03-23","url":"https://www.who.int/thailand/news/detail/23-03-2022-thailand-s-migrant-vaccination-programme-ensures-no-one-is-left-behind","claim":"Thailand initiated an inclusive policy to provide free COVID-19 vaccines to documented and undocumented migrant workers and expats, and the joint project had vaccinated 1,186 migrants while using outreach, language support, mobile services, and relief packages.","locator":"paras. 62-72 and 77-85","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO reports a direct government-linked health access intervention for a vulnerable group with specific operational details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Strong 2022 fit; the article is contemporaneous with implementation in March 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Thai government under Prayut through the Ministry of Public Health and partner agencies, though the execution also depended on WHO/IOM/WVF and local actors.","contrary_evidence":["The project addresses a vulnerable-group gap rather than proving comprehensive inclusion across all social services.","Undocumented migrants still face documentation and access barriers."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand Public Spending and Revenue Assessment: Promoting an Inclusive and Sustainable Future","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-06","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/thailand/publication/th-prsa","claim":"The report frames Thailand's spending choices as needing to support an inclusive and sustainable economy, with specific attention to health, education, and social protection spending that is responsive to vulnerable groups.","locator":"report overview and chapter summaries","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank country assessment summarizing its spending priorities for Thailand.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Published after the target year but explicitly about Thailand's spending structure and welfare priorities relevant to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is indirect: this is a diagnostic of the Prayut-era fiscal and social-spending environment, not a direct judgment of his personal actions.","contrary_evidence":["The page itself is a high-level overview, so it does not prove delivery or outcomes.","Detailed conclusions still depend on the chapter PDFs and survey data."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand's health system chapter in Thailand Public Revenue and Spending Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-06","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099052523201027923/pdf/P1771570af8b360b40aab305f0be65a2678.pdf","claim":"Thailand achieved remarkable population-health improvements, and those gains were anchored in long-standing policy action toward universal health coverage and primary health care, with comprehensive benefit packages and financial risk protection across the three public health insurance schemes.","locator":"p.3, paras. 157-158 and p.17, para. 183","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank chapter with direct health-system analysis and quantitative health indicators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Report is 2023, but it analyzes the health system as it existed through 2022 and immediately before.","ruler_attribution":"Only partly attributable to Prayut: the report explicitly says the gains rest on long-standing government policy and action, so this is more baseline/inherited capacity than a Prayut-only achievement.","contrary_evidence":["The same chapter notes urban-rural disparities, bypassing of primary care in cities, and future aging pressures.","This is a system-strength claim, not proof of 2022 discretionary reform."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand's education spending chapter in Thailand Public Revenue and Spending Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-06","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099052523201515270/pdf/P17715706b8dee0540b1d10b6c2c1a8bd5c.pdf","claim":"Despite higher real per-student spending, learning outcomes worsened; in 2020, 57 percent of OBEC schools were small and about 19 percent of students were enrolled in them, indicating an inefficient and inequitable school structure.","locator":"p.12-13, paras. 236-237","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank analysis uses school-level data and directly ties spending to outcomes and school structure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Analyzes the 2020 school system and the longer-run policy pattern feeding into 2022 conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is partial and often structural: it reflects the Prayut-era policy environment and inherited education system, not a single 2022 decision.","contrary_evidence":["The chapter is diagnostic rather than an audit of a specific ministerial reform.","It highlights inefficiency and inequality more than direct negligence or improvement."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand's social protection chapter in Thailand Public Revenue and Spending Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-06","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099052523201584503/pdf/P17715706c644a0660bbef030a8f79a55f5.pdf","claim":"Thailand's social protection system is fragmented and inequitable, over half of employed people lack any coverage, and old-age allowance benefits have stagnated and fallen below the poverty line.","locator":"p.2, paras. 281-283","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct country-system analysis by the World Bank with clear welfare and coverage metrics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The report is 2023 but is explicitly describing the 2022-era welfare system and its pressures.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the Prayut-led government, but also strongly shaped by inherited informality, aging, and prior scheme design.","contrary_evidence":["The report also says the system had high coverage of some assistance benefits, so the picture is not uniformly negative.","This describes system weakness more than a single ruler decision."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand's social protection chapter in Thailand Public Revenue and Spending Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-06","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099052523201584503/pdf/P17715706c644a0660bbef030a8f79a55f5.pdf","claim":"The government responded to COVID-19 with a significant social assistance package, including top-up transfers and new programs for informal workers and farmers, and over 80 percent of households received some form of assistance; the rollout showed the value of Thailand's e-welfare infrastructure.","locator":"p.2, paras. 284-285","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific, measurable description of crisis-response delivery and the administrative infrastructure behind it.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Describes the pandemic response that was still shaping 2022 social policy.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut-led government and Thai administrative systems, though the package was also a response to an external shock.","contrary_evidence":["The chapter says major programs still spread limited spending too thin, diluting impact.","This is a crisis-response positive, not proof of durable institutional transformation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand's social protection chapter in Thailand Public Revenue and Spending Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-06","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099052523201584503/pdf/P17715706c644a0660bbef030a8f79a55f5.pdf","claim":"Almost three quarters of the population received some form of social assistance in 2019 and 95 percent of the poorest quintile received at least one benefit, but the World Bank still judged the spending pattern inefficient and the social insurance system weak.","locator":"p.3, paras. 286-287","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Quantitative welfare coverage statement from an official World Bank diagnostic.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The figures are pre-2022 but are the baseline Thailand entered 2022 with.","ruler_attribution":"Baseline-heavy and only partly attributable to Prayut; the numbers mostly capture inherited structure and distribution rather than a single-year action.","contrary_evidence":["High coverage does not mean high adequacy; the same source calls the spending pattern inefficient.","The high coverage is partly a legacy of earlier schemes and not necessarily a 2022 accomplishment."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand's social protection chapter in Thailand Public Revenue and Spending Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-06","url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099052523201584503/pdf/P17715706c644a0660bbef030a8f79a55f5.pdf","claim":"Without social assistance transfers, Thailand's 2019 poverty headcount would have been 22 percent higher and the poverty gap almost 38 percent higher, showing material poverty-reduction effects from the system.","locator":"p.5, paras. 291 and surrounding discussion","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct quantitative estimate from a reputable development report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 baseline evidence that remained relevant to the 2022 target year.","ruler_attribution":"This supports the Prayut-era welfare record only partially because the effect reflects the existing system, not a ruler-specific 2022 reform.","contrary_evidence":["The same chapter stresses leakage, fragmentation, and low benefit levels.","Poverty reduction effect does not by itself show broad durability or equitable design."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rural Thailand Faces the Largest Poverty Challenges with High Income Inequality","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/21/rural-thailand-faces-the-largest-poverty-challenges-with-high-income-inequality","claim":"Thailand reduced poverty from 58 percent in 1990 to 6.8 percent in 2020, but 79 percent of the poor remained in rural areas and poverty reduction slowed after 2015, with the South and Northeast especially disadvantaged.","locator":"paras. 144-147","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank press release with explicit poverty and geographic-distribution data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during the target year and describing the distributional baseline entering late 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Prayut-era policy and macro conditions, but strongly shaped by inherited rural inequality and the COVID shock.","contrary_evidence":["The same source acknowledges long-run poverty reduction gains.","This is a distributional criticism, not proof of complete welfare failure."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Thailand","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/06/13/pr22195-imf-staff-completes-2022-article-iv-mission-to-thailand","claim":"IMF staff said remaining programs should be targeted to protect hard-hit and vulnerable segments, and recommended structural reforms to improve social outcomes through more investment in education, ICT, green-resilient infrastructure, and expanded social protection coverage.","locator":"paras. 247-260","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous IMF staff statement with concrete policy advice and target-group language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous with 2022 policy choices.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut-led authorities as the policy interlocutor, though the statement is IMF advice and not proof of full implementation.","contrary_evidence":["The same statement says the outlook was uncertain and inflation was rising, limiting what policy could achieve.","Advisory recommendations are not the same as executed reforms."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"แนวทางพัฒนา “โรงเรียนคุณภาพ” ตามนโยบายกระทรวงศึกษาธิการ","publisher":"Ministry of Education, Thailand","publication_date":"2022-08-26","url":"https://www.moe.go.th/360guidelines-developing-quality/","claim":"The ministry's quality-school policy aimed to improve 12,938 schools over four five-year phases, with seven focus areas including teacher supply, quality assurance, instruction, assessment, supervision, tracking, and Big Data, while explicitly aiming to expand opportunity and reduce inequality.","locator":"paras. 506-514","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry policy statement with specific implementation structure, but it is still a policy announcement rather than a delivery audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and squarely within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut-era cabinet and education ministry, but the article describes a ministerial program rather than a direct PM order.","contrary_evidence":["The source is aspirational and long-term, so it does not prove 2022 delivery results.","It does not establish whether funding and staffing targets were actually met."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I screened roughly 30 documents across UNICEF/WHO/World Bank/IMF/Thai ministry sources, plus Reuters mirrors and local-government press releases.

Accepted as evidence:
- UNICEF MICS 2022 press release and UNICEF pandemic-impact article.
- WHO migrant vaccination feature.
- World Bank Thailand Public Revenue and Spending Assessment page and its health, education, and social-protection chapters.
- World Bank rural poverty diagnostic.
- IMF June 2022 Article IV mission statement.
- Thai Ministry of Education school-quality policy page.

Rejected or not used:
- Duplicate landing pages for the same UNICEF MICS report and the same World Bank assessment were dropped in favor of the more specific press release or chapter PDFs.
- A few IMF eLibrary and ministry pages failed to fetch or returned 403/500-style errors, so they were not relied on.
- Several 2024-2026 Thai PRD and ministry items surfaced in search but were out of period for 2022 and were excluded.
- Generic global UNICEF/WHO reports and later follow-on stories were rejected when they added no Thailand-2022-specific ruler-period evidence.
- Historical background notes and technical notes from 2017-2021 were treated as background only unless a 2022-targeted claim was available from a stronger source.

**Remaining gaps**
- The biggest gap is direct 2022 ruler-specific attribution: there is still limited evidence that Prayut personally designed, ordered, or corrected the social-welfare and education measures rather than inheriting them through Thailand's existing systems.
- More search would help on 2022 Thai-language budget execution, welfare-card administration, and formal ministry reporting on social protection uptake, especially for poor regions, children, migrants, and older people.
- The full NSO/UNICEF MICS 2022 report PDF would likely sharpen province-level and group-level attribution beyond the press-release summary.
- Independent audits or implementation reviews of the 2022 education reforms and social protection targeting would help distinguish announced policy from actual delivery.
